{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LWqUsCftpFR1X4vgXfD9xG","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect. Now I have enough information. Let me compile my findings:\n\n## Architectural Friction Points — Almanac Codebase\n\nBased on my thorough analysis of the Almanac codebase, here are the concrete friction points I've identified:\n\n### 1. **Thin Pass-Through Role Wrappers (harden-core.sh:61–69, converge-core.sh:56–61)**\nBoth `almanac_harden_role_field` and `almanac_converge_role_field` are near-identical thin wrappers that call their respective role readers and pipe to an identical awk invocation. Interface complexity equals implementation complexity; deletion would concentrate this 9-line pattern into a shared generic `almanac_loop_role_field(loop_name, role, field, lens)` in `lib/role.sh`.\n\n**Deletion test result:** Consolidation would reduce duplication and earn its interface by becoming a genuine cross-loop utility.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Duplicated Convergence Loop Logic (harden-core.sh:1577–1717, converge-core.sh:850–986)**\n`almanac_harden_run` (140 lines) and `almanac_converge_run` (136 lines) both implement the same convergence skeleton: register the run, trap finalization, loop under a budget, consume stop/steer signals between rounds, call a round handler, check a gate verdict, and dispatch on continue/converged/budget-hit. The gate logic (`almanac_harden_gate_verdict`, 18 lines) is harden-specific but the loop structure and signal consumption pattern are identical. Deletion of duplication would require extracting a shared loop driver that accepts a round-handler callback.\n\n**Deletion test result:** A unified convergence driver function would eliminate ~250 lines of duplicate orchestration logic without losing semantic intent.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **God-Module Harden-Core (1979 lines): Shallow Rubric Subsystem**\nRubric operations (lines 71–184: write, approve, snapshot, verify) form a cohesive file-and-markdown subsystem nearly as narrow as their interface. They are logically independent of reviewer fan-out, ratification, fixing, or dashboard logic — each could live in its own module (e.g., `lib/harden-rubric.sh`). The only integration is path construction via `almanac_harden_rubric_path` (3 calls at lines 86, 134, 1023, …). Deletion of the rubric subsystem from harden-core would not reduce overall complexity; it would decompose a monolithic module into testable submodules.\n\n**Deletion test result:** Extracting to a separate file concentrates testability and reduces the per-file token overhead of harden-core.sh.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **God-Module Harden-Core: Shallow Findings-Ledger Subsystem**\nThe ledger (lines 459–773) — TSV-based append-only ledger for ratified findings — is another cohesive subsystem orthogonal to the feedback loops and rubric. It owns parsing, deduplication, and status updates but is invoked only by ratification (almanac_harden_ratify, line 902) and reporting. The 315-line subsystem could live as `lib/harden-findings.sh`. Deletion would not simplify harden-core; it would partition it.\n\n**Deletion test result:** Extraction to `lib/harden-findings.sh` isolates a self-contained contract and shrinks harden-core's cognitive footprint.\n\n---\n\n### 5. **No Seam Violation: But Leaky Integration with Run Registry (run.sh:939–1007)**\n`almanac_loop_new_run_argv` and `almanac_loop_new_run_env` branch internally on loop type (lines 962, 974, 979, 1026, 1034) to emit ralph/harden/converge-specific config. This violates the loop-adapter seam: the registry (lib/run.sh) should not know loop-specific launch signatures. The adapter's `launch` verb should yield (argv, env) pairs; the launcher should not reassemble them. CONTEXT.md claims adapters are auto-discovered with no central branching on loop type — but run.sh violates this at the hub's new-run composition layer.\n\n**Deletion test result:** Moving argv/env composition to the adapter's `launch` verb and adding a `new_run_composition` contract would delete the 70-line case-statement and push config encoding into each adapter.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **Untested Rubric Immutability Guard (harden-core.sh:275–305)**\n`almanac_harden_rubric_snapshot` and `almanac_harden_rubric_verify` are snapshot/restore helpers bracketing agent phases to prevent rubric edits. No dedicated test file exists for harden-core logic — `tests/test-harden-cli.sh` only tests CLI integration, not the core module. The immutability pattern is correctness-critical (the contract is the goalposts) yet untested outside live runs.\n\n**Deletion test result:** A new `tests/test-harden-core.sh` would force the interface into explicit, testable form; deletion would reveal whether the pattern is truly necessary or cargo-culted.\n\n---\n\n### 7. **Duplicated Role Resolution Pattern (harden-core.sh:45–56, converge-core.sh:45–54)**\n`almanac_harden_role` (11 lines) and `almanac_converge_role` both validate a role, clear the lens for single-instance roles, then dispatch to `almanac_loop_role_config`. The pattern is a thin lens-filtering wrapper over the shared resolver. A generic `almanac_loop_role(loop, role, lens)` in `lib/role.sh` would eliminate duplication; each loop could validate roles via a loop-specific validation function (not a central case-statement).\n\n**Deletion test result:** Consolidation into `lib/role.sh` + per-loop validation hooks would unify 22 identical lines into one generic call.\n\n---\n\n### 8. **Converge-Core Worker Dispatch: Inline Prompt vs. Exec Mode (converge-core.sh:600–636, 710–792)**\n`almanac_converge_run_worker` and `almanac_converge_run_worker_prompt` are two nearly-identical worker launchers (60 + 80 lines) that differ only in how they construct the prompt (inline exec vs. user-provided prompt) and their auto-commit strategy. Both resolve roles identically, both call `almanac_loop_agent_stream`, both write agent-reports.log. The mode dispatch (lines 850–867) chooses which to call. A unified worker launcher accepting a `(prompt_builder, mode)` pair would eliminate ~100 lines of duplication.\n\n**Deletion test result:** Refactoring to a single worker function with a configurable prompt-builder callback would reduce duplication and clarify the two modes as parameter variations, not separate functions.\n\n---\n\n### 9. **Run-Registry Type Branching Violates Loop Seam (run.sh:1026–1041)**\n`almanac_loop_new_run_env` (lines 1013–1042) branches on loop type to emit environment variables (HARDEN_LENSES, HARDEN_PROVIDER, CONVERGE_PROVIDER, etc.). The hub should not know loop-specific environment prefixes. Each loop adapter should declare its own env schema; the registry should call a per-adapter hook (e.g., `almanac_loop_<name>_new_run_env`) instead of branching.\n\n**Deletion test result:** Moving env composition to adapters and adding a `new_run_env` contract would delete the 24-line case-statement and centralize loop config knowledge in the adapter.\n\n---\n\n### 10. **Shallow Convergence Dashboard Subsystem (harden-core.sh:1719–1949)**\nThe supervision dashboard (230 lines: rows composition, worker health aggregation, redraw loop) is orthogonal to the convergence algorithm. It reads worker status and renders it, with no feedback into the loop logic. Lines 1759–1787 are a pure presentation layer; lines 1793–1815 are just status gathering. Extraction to `lib/harden-dashboard.sh` would partition concerns without complexity loss.\n\n**Deletion test result:** Extraction isolates the UI/reporting concern and shrinks harden-core to its semantic core (rubric/ledger/convergence/ratification logic).\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n**Five actionable patterns:**\n\n1. **Identical role-field wrappers** (harden/converge) → consolidate to shared `lib/role.sh` utility.\n2. **Duplicated convergence loop skeleton** (harden/converge) → extract shared driver with callback.\n3. **Type-branching in run-registry** (lines 962, 1026 in run.sh) → move argv/env composition to adapter contracts.\n4. **God-module harden-core (1979 lines)** → partition rubric/ledger/dashboard into separate focused modules.\n5. **Untested core logic** (harden-core ratification, rubric immutability) → create `tests/test-harden-core.sh` to surface seams.\n\nNone require design change; all are decomposition and seam enforcement at existing boundaries."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f9a4bfcb-3f60-4000-92ad-9c030057e806","uuid":"997aebf3-31f1-4303-bcca-7102ed30b862","timestamp":"2026-05-26T20:37:56.755Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Explore the Almanac codebase at /Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac for architectural friction points. The codebase is a bash CLI for orchestrating AI agent loops (ralph/harden/converge) with auto-discovered loop and provider adapters.\n\nDomain vocabulary (CONTEXT.md):\n- **Loop**: ralph/harden/converge. Adding a loop = adding an adapter at lib/loops/<name>.sh.\n- **Provider**: codex or claude, adapters at lib/providers/<name>.sh.\n- **Loop adapter contract**: launch verbs (launch/launch_usage/exec_argv) and control (signal_file).\n- **Run registry**: .almanac/runs/, with run-status record + worker-status record, both via generic TSV record engine in lib/run.sh.\n\nBig files to scrutinise:\n- lib/harden-core.sh (1979 lines)\n- lib/run.sh (1042 lines)\n- lib/converge-core.sh (986 lines)\n\nKey questions for each:\n1. **Shallow modules** — interface nearly as complex as implementation (apply deletion test: would deletion concentrate complexity, or just move it?).\n2. **Pass-through wrappers** — thin functions that just rename or forward args without earning their interface.\n3. **Cross-module duplication** — harden-core and converge-core both manage convergence loops, both fan out workers, both handle records. Is there shared shape that's been re-implemented in two places?\n4. **Leaky seams** — modules that branch on loop type or provider name outside the adapter (CONTEXT.md claims 