nes into one generic call.\n\n---\n\n### 8. **Converge-Core Worker Dispatch: Inline Prompt vs. Exec Mode (converge-core.sh:600–636, 710–792)**\n`almanac_converge_run_worker` and `almanac_converge_run_worker_prompt` are two nearly-identical worker launchers (60 + 80 lines) that differ only in how they construct the prompt (inline exec vs. user-provided prompt) and their auto-commit strategy. Both resolve roles identically, both call `almanac_loop_agent_stream`, both write agent-reports.log. The mode dispatch (lines 850–867) chooses which to call. A unified worker launcher accepting a `(prompt_builder, mode)` pair would eliminate ~100 lines of duplication.\n\n**Deletion test result:** Refactoring to a single worker function with a configurable prompt-builder callback would reduce duplication and clarify the two modes as parameter variations, not separate functions.\n\n---\n\n### 9. **Run-Registry Type Branching Violates Loop Seam (run.sh:1026–1041)**\n`almanac_loop_new_run_env` (lines 1013–1042) branches on loop type to emit environment variables (HARDEN_LENSES, HARDEN_PROVIDER, CONVERGE_PROVIDER, etc.). The hub should not know loop-specific environment prefixes. Each loop adapter should declare its own env schema; the registry should call a per-adapter hook (e.g., `almanac_loop_<name>_new_run_env`) instead of branching.\n\n**Deletion test result:** Moving env composition to adapters and adding a `new_run_env` contract would delete the 24-line case-statement and centralize loop config knowledge in the adapter.\n\n---\n\n### 10. **Shallow Convergence Dashboard Subsystem (harden-core.sh:1719–1949)**\nThe supervision dashboard (230 lines: rows composition, worker health aggregation, redraw loop) is orthogonal to the convergence algorithm. It reads worker status and renders it, with no feedback into the loop logic. Lines 1759–1787 are a pure presentation layer; lines 1793–1815 are just status gathering. Extraction to `lib/harden-dashboard.sh` would partition concerns with