_loop_ui_input`/`almanac_loop_ui_confirm` seam, so the same menus work on any TTY. Off a TTY (piped/captured) the hub still renders the read-only overview once and exits. The **new-run** flow is itself built on a pure composer + non-interactive seam, mirroring the per-run actions: `almanac_loop_new_run_argv <type> [key=value…]` composes the launcher argv (one token per line, leading with the `ralph`/`harden` subcommand) — ralph config maps to ralph.sh flags (`--prd`/`--mode`/`--provider`/`--model`/`--effort`/`--iterations`/`--no-oversee`), harden launches the convergence loop (`harden <target> --loop [--rounds N]`); `almanac_loop_new_run_env <type> [key=value…]` composes the `KEY=VALUE` env a run needs beyond its argv (ralph emits none — all flags; harden's reviewer config rides on `HARDEN_LENSES`/`HARDEN_PROVIDER`/`HARDEN_MODEL`/`HARDEN_EFFORT`). Both return 1 for an unknown type and 2 for a missing required field (ralph: prd; harden: target), so a malformed run is rejected rather than launched. `cmd/hub.sh`'s `almanac_hub_launch_new` either prints the resolved `ENV… almanac <argv>` command (dry-run, the scripts-safe preview the gum confirm reuses) or exports the env and execs the launcher, handing over the terminal. The interactive New-run flow (`almanac_hub_new_run`) picks the loop type, then hands off to the shared **almanac launcher** (`almanac_loop_launch`, see the CLI section) — no run-config logic lives in the hub any more; the same launcher backs `almanac ralph` / `almanac harden`, so the New-run UX is identical to running those directly. (The non-interactive `--new` composer seam below stays for scripts/tests.) The seam is also drivable directly: `almanac hub --new <ralph|harden> [config…] [--dry-run]`. `tests/test-hub.sh` pins the non-interactive seams (`--steer` writes the steer file with the directive, `--stop` writes the stop file, `--watch` renders the run detail once off a TTY, `--new … --dry-run` composes the ralph/harden launch and rejects a run missing its required config). The argv/env composers themselves do **not** branch on loop type — each loop adapter implements `new_run_argv` / `new_run_env` as additional contract verbs (alongside `launch` / `launch_usage` / `exec_argv`), and `almanac_loop_new_run_argv` / `_env` in `lib/run.sh` are thin dispatchers that validate the loop name (`almanac_loop_adapter_known` → 1 for unknown) and forward the key=value stream through `almanac_loop_adapter_call`. Adding a 4th loop adds the two composer verbs to its adapter; the dispatcher does not change."," "," The `harden-loop` skill (`skills/loop/harden-loop/SKILL.md`) is the agent-facing companion to the harden CLI - it teaches the convergence contract, the round structure, lenses, per-role provider config, supervision, and gum-optional degradation, pointing at `almanac harden <target>`. The `converge-loop` skill (`skills/loop/converge-loop/SKILL.md`) is the companion to `almanac converge` - it teaches the generic goal/exec loop, mutable goal state, overseer contract, role config, supervision, and when to choose converge instead of ralph or harden. Like `ralph-loop`, both are `disable-model-invocation: true` heavyweight autonomous loops: user-invocable via `/almanac:harden-loop` or `/almanac:converge-loop`, and loadable by orchestrators without bloating the auto-listing."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
